1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,700
Scenes with thin lines, as in INSIDE,

2
00:00:02,700 --> 00:00:06,160
can have shimmering edges in motion.

3
00:00:06,160 --> 00:00:08,580
An image consists of small cells.

4
00:00:08,580 --> 00:00:11,140
Each cell contains one color.

5
00:00:11,140 --> 00:00:12,340
A cell is a pixel;

6
00:00:12,340 --> 00:00:15,840
one complete image is a frame.

7
00:00:15,840 --> 00:00:17,300
INSIDE adds information from earlier images

8
00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:19,220
to its current image.

9
00:00:19,220 --> 00:00:21,260
But simply overlaying the old image

10
00:00:21,260 --> 00:00:23,900
can leave ghosts behind moving objects.

11
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:26,380
A thin branch can fall between sample positions,

12
00:00:26,380 --> 00:00:29,460
appearing in one image and missing the next.

13
00:00:29,460 --> 00:00:31,640
The object can stay the same

14
00:00:31,640 --> 00:00:33,960
while its thin edge appears to flicker.

15
00:00:33,960 --> 00:00:37,060
Each frame samples a different location inside the pixel.

16
00:00:37,060 --> 00:00:38,540
This is called jitter.

17
00:00:38,540 --> 00:00:40,600
One image can capture thin-line information

18
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:42,360
that another image missed.

19
00:00:42,360 --> 00:00:43,540
Combining those images carefully

20
00:00:43,540 --> 00:00:46,460
can make the edges more stable.

21
00:00:46,460 --> 00:00:48,240
Think of sampling the left side of a cell first,

22
00:00:48,240 --> 00:00:50,880
then its right side in the next image.

23
00:00:50,880 --> 00:00:53,000
Combining those different samples

24
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:55,760
gives a better estimate of the thin edge.

25
00:00:55,760 --> 00:00:59,200
A moving camera or object changes its screen position.

26
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:01,860
Use depth and motion information together.

27
00:01:01,860 --> 00:01:05,500
Find where the same point was in the previous frame.

28
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:08,240
This finds the corresponding position in the old frame.

29
00:01:08,240 --> 00:01:09,640
If the boy moves right,

30
00:01:09,640 --> 00:01:13,780
mixing his old position in place leaves a ghost.

31
00:01:13,780 --> 00:01:15,580
First align the corresponding position,

32
00:01:15,580 --> 00:01:17,760
then bring in the earlier information.

33
00:01:17,760 --> 00:01:21,340
When a hidden area appears, old information may be wrong.

34
00:01:21,340 --> 00:01:24,160
Check the color range around the current pixel.

35
00:01:24,160 --> 00:01:27,280
Bring incompatible past colors within that range.

36
00:01:27,280 --> 00:01:30,460
This helps prevent incorrect traces from persisting.

37
00:01:30,460 --> 00:01:32,260
A background revealed behind the boy

38
00:01:32,260 --> 00:01:34,480
does not need his old color.

39
00:01:34,480 --> 00:01:36,760
If the current neighborhood is dark,

40
00:01:36,760 --> 00:01:39,680
a bright historical color should not be trusted unchanged.

41
00:01:39,680 --> 00:01:42,520
Blend the adjusted history with the current color

42
00:01:42,520 --> 00:01:45,360
and store the result for the next frame.

43
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:48,660
Fast motion also uses separate motion blur.

44
00:01:48,660 --> 00:01:51,460
That blur is not repeatedly accumulated in history.

45
00:01:51,460 --> 00:01:53,080
Stabilizing an edge and blurring fast motion

46
00:01:53,080 --> 00:01:56,660
serve different purposes.

47
00:01:56,660 --> 00:01:58,200
Repeatedly accumulating an already blurred result

48
00:01:58,200 --> 00:02:00,360
could blur later images further.

49
00:02:00,360 --> 00:02:03,100
Gather screen information from slightly different positions.

50
00:02:03,100 --> 00:02:06,140
Find the same point and correct its past color.

51
00:02:06,140 --> 00:02:08,580
Blend that information with the current color.

52
00:02:08,580 --> 00:02:11,960
Choose only information that fits the current image.

53
00:02:11,960 --> 00:02:13,120
Using time means

54
00:02:13,120 --> 00:02:16,120
using information collected across several images.
